<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!--
      LS0001 ("LearnStackException-DomainExceptionThrow", ADR-0032
      § Sub-decision 4 + Amendment 1) is a Warning until the Phase 03 exit
      gate, then escalates to Error. Keep it OUT of TreatWarningsAsErrors
      until then so a legitimate aggregate-invariant `throw new
      DomainException(...)` does not break CI before the documented
      escalation point. Remove LS0001 from this list when the analyzer's
      default severity flips to Error after Phase 03 exit.
    -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);LS0001</WarningsNotAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <!--
      Analyzer posture: `Recommended` is the Microsoft-curated middle ground
      (~120 rules) — security + correctness + reliability + maintainability
      defaults that the .NET team considers the floor for production code.
      `AllEnabledByDefault` (~450 rules) was the prior setting; it tripped
      every Phase 01 PR on cosmetic / xunit-pattern rules that drowned out
      the signal. The rules we specifically want UPGRADED to error (e.g.
      security-sensitive CAs, our own architecture-test contract) get an
      explicit `dotnet_diagnostic.<id>.severity = error` line in the
      relevant `.editorconfig` — that posture promotes signal over churn.

      NOTE: this analyzer posture is currently a tooling choice, NOT a
      corpus-anchored rule. The first ADR amendment that pins the
      analyzer mode + the per-rule promotion mechanism should land in
      Phase 02a (most likely as an ADR-0032 amendment, since the
      cross-cutting concerns story owns developer-experience policy).
    -->
    <AnalysisMode>Recommended</AnalysisMode>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1014;CS1591</NoWarn>
    <!--
      NuGet audit over the WHOLE graph, transitive packages included. `direct` was the
      setting until Packet 9's review round, on the reasoning that a dev-only test
      library's transitive advisories were not ours to fix — and it hid three high-
      severity advisories for months: Microsoft.OpenApi 2.0.0 (through
      Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi, in the API), System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 9.0.0
      (through EF Core Design's MSBuild) and SSH.NET 2023.0.0 (through Testcontainers).
      Security Standards § Dependency Hygiene patches a high advisory within seven days,
      which a build that never reports one cannot do. Under CI's TreatWarningsAsErrors a
      new advisory of ANY severity fails the build — NuGetAuditLevel is stated as `low`
      below rather than inherited, because "high" was what the prose around it used to
      say: move the package that brings it to a patched release, and if none exists yet,
      suppress that one advisory with NuGetAuditSuppress beside a dated reason — never by
      narrowing this mode or raising that level again.

      NU1900 — the vulnerability data could not be fetched — fails CI the same way, and
      deliberately: a restore that could not check the graph is not a clean one. Re-run
      when the feed is back; do not demote the warning.
    -->
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <RootNamespace>$(MSBuildProjectName)</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>$(MSBuildProjectName)</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(IsTestProject)' == 'true'">
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!--
      Analyzer rules that don't fit xunit test code conventions:
        CA1707 — `Method_When_Returns` underscore naming is THE xunit
                 convention; renaming every test would obscure the BDD shape.
        CA1812 — xunit instantiates test fixtures via reflection; the
                 analyzer can't see those callsites and flags every
                 IClassFixture target as dead code.
        CA1515 — xunit's runner discovers `public` classes; suppressing here
                 lets test types stay public without re-flagging.
        CA1034 — nested test types (e.g. nested theory data classes) are an
                 accepted xunit pattern; the rule is intended for library
                 APIs, not test scaffolding.
        CA2234 — `HttpClient.GetAsync(string)` overload is fine in test
                 assertions; the Uri-overload preference is library-grade
                 hygiene that costs readability in tests.
        CA1711 — xunit collection-definition classes by convention end in
                 "Collection"; the rule was written for API surfaces, not
                 test scaffolding.
        CA1822 — controller actions / endpoint methods that intentionally
                 throw for L1-handler integration tests don't access
                 instance data; making them static is fine but
                 ControllerBase conventions and ASP.NET routing prefer
                 instance methods.
      Real code-quality rules (CA1305 culture-invariant formatting, CA1861
      static-readonly array reuse) STAY ON — fix violations in the code.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1707;CA1812;CA1515;CA1034;CA2234;CA1711;CA1822</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
